# config.env.example — your own settings, in one file.
#
# HOW TO USE THIS FILE
# Copy it, from the repo root, to a file called config.env:
#     cp config.env.example config.env
# Then open config.env in any text editor and remove the "#" in front of the
# lines you want to change. A line that still starts with "#" does nothing.
#
# config.env is listed in .gitignore, so it never goes into a git repository.
# That matters because API_KEY below is a password.
#
# HONEST WARNING: bin/env.sh RUNS this file as a shell script. It does not
# merely read KEY=value pairs out of it. Put nothing in here except KEY=value
# lines and comments, and never paste a command you found on the internet.
#
# WHERE A SETTING COMES FROM — highest priority first:
#   1. a real environment variable    CTX_SIZE=32768 ./bin/serve.sh
#   2. this file
#   3. what bin/detect-hardware.sh measures about your Mac
#   4. the built-in default in bin/env.sh
#
# You do not have to set anything. Every line below has a working default, and
# the four memory settings are worked out from your Mac's own size.
# To see what your Mac was measured as, run this from the repo root:
#     ./bin/detect-hardware.sh


# --- Which model, and where -------------------------------------------------

# The address on huggingface.co that ./bin/download-model.sh clones.
# If the download tool says the name is not found, open the model's page on
# huggingface.co, copy the "<organization>/<name>" text at the top, and put it
# here.
#MODEL_REPO=chimingw/Qwen3.8-27B-Uncensored-OrcaRouter-MLX-5bit

# Which build of the checkpoint to use: 4bit, 5bit or 8bit. All three exist.
# Left alone, bin/detect-hardware.sh picks the largest one your Mac has memory
# for, and MODEL_REPO and MODEL_DIR follow that choice. Weights in memory,
# text-only: 4-bit about 16.3 GB, 5-bit about 19.1 GB, 8-bit about 27.7 GB.
#MODEL_QUANT=5bit

# The folder on your Mac that holds the weights (the model's numbers: about
# 20 GB on disk for the 5-bit build, of which about 19.1 GB is loaded into
# memory). Must be a full path. Replace <YOUR_USER_NAME> with your own account
# name, the one that appears in the output of the command `whoami`.
# By default this follows MODEL_REPO, so the folder is always named after what
# is inside it.
#MODEL_DIR=/Users/<YOUR_USER_NAME>/dev/local-llms/airgap/Qwen3.8-27B-Uncensored-OrcaRouter-MLX-5bit

# The name the server answers to. By default it is the folder name above, and
# that is what the server really uses. Change the folder name and this changes
# with it. There is almost never a reason to set this by hand.
#MODEL_ID=Qwen3.8-27B-Uncensored-OrcaRouter-MLX-5bit


# --- The server -------------------------------------------------------------

# Which network the server listens on. 127.0.0.1 means "this Mac only".
# DO NOT CHANGE THIS. The server's own default is 0.0.0.0, which means every
# network your Mac is connected to. This model has had its refusal behavior
# removed, and the server has no password unless you set API_KEY below.
#HOST=127.0.0.1

# Which numbered door on your Mac the server listens at. Change it only if
# something else is already using 11234.
#PORT=11234

# A password for the server. Empty means no password, which is correct while
# HOST stays 127.0.0.1. If you set this, ./bin/claude-local.sh uses it too.
# It is NOT a secret: it is passed on a command line, so every other account on
# this Mac can read it with `ps`. It is a second lock on a door already inside
# your house.
#API_KEY=

# 1 publishes speed and cache counters. See docs/07-tuning.md.
#METRICS=1

# How much detail the server writes to its log file. One of: error warn info debug
#LOG_LEVEL=info

# Where the server writes its log file.
#LOG_FILE=/Users/<YOUR_USER_NAME>/.mlx-serve/logs/mlx-serve-11234.log

# Passed to the server exactly as you type it, after every other setting.
# An escape hatch for a setting this repo has not given a name to.
# Six flags are refused outright and bin/serve.sh will not start with them here:
# --host, --lan-share, --lan-discover, --skip-mem-preflight, --no-mtp, --no-pld.
#EXTRA_ARGS=


# --- Memory and size (worked out from your Mac by default) ------------------
# On the test machine (Apple M3 Max, 36 GB unified memory) detection produces
# CTX_SIZE=65536, MIN_FREE_GB=22, MAX_RESIDENT_MEM=21GB, PREFIX_CACHE_MEM=1536MB.
# Your Mac will produce different numbers. To see yours, run from the repo root:
#     ./bin/detect-hardware.sh
# Read docs/04-memory-safety.md before you raise any of them.

# How much text the model can hold at once, counted in tokens (a token is
# roughly three quarters of a word). The model's own maximum is 262144.
# Bigger costs memory: every 65536 tokens costs about 1 GB.
#CTX_SIZE=65536

# The server refuses to start when less than this many GB of memory is free.
# This is what turns "my Mac froze" into a message you can read. Setting it to
# 0 switches the check off and is not advised.
#MIN_FREE_GB=22

# The most memory the loaded model is allowed to occupy.
#MAX_RESIDENT_MEM=21GB

# Memory kept aside to remember Claude Code's repeated instructions, so they do
# not have to be re-read every turn. This is the biggest speed win there is.
#PREFIX_CACHE_MEM=1536MB

# The same thing on your SSD, so it survives a server restart. Costs disk, not
# memory.
#PREFIX_CACHE_DISK=10GB

# Seconds of silence after which the server hands the ~19.1 GB back to macOS.
# The next question then waits about a minute for a reload. Set 0 to keep the
# model in memory all the time.
#IDLE_EVICT_SECS=900

# How much of your text the server reads at a time on the first pass. Smaller
# means a smaller temporary memory spike, at a small speed cost.
#PREFILL_CHUNK=4096

# How the running conversation is stored. turbo4 is four bits per number after
# a rotation that spreads the extreme values out first. Raise to 8 if answers
# get worse in long conversations. See docs/06-troubleshooting.md.
#KV_QUANT=turbo4

# 1 skips loading the part of the model that reads images. Claude Code sends
# text, so skipping it saves memory. Set 0 only to feed the model pictures.
#NO_VISION=1

# One model, one copy in memory.
#MAX_RESIDENT_MODELS=1


# --- Downloading ------------------------------------------------------------

# ./bin/download-model.sh refuses to start with less than this many GB free.
# The peak is about double the final size, because the download tool keeps a
# second copy of every file until the last step reclaims it.
#MIN_DISK_GB=45

# 1 makes the last download step reclaim that second copy (~19 GB back,
# instantly, with no data loss). Set 0 only if you want to keep it.
#DEDUP=1


# --- Claude Code ------------------------------------------------------------

# 1 starts Claude Code with its extra tool servers switched off.
# Measured on the test machine: Claude Code's own instructions are 20,909
# tokens that way, and 38,054 tokens with every tool server loaded. Those tool
# descriptions cost about 17,000 tokens on EVERY turn. Set 0 to load your
# normal configuration instead.
#LEAN_MCP=1

# The command that starts Claude Code. Change it if yours is somewhere unusual.
#CLAUDE_BIN=claude

# The longest single answer Claude Code will ask for, in tokens.
#CLAUDE_CODE_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS=8192


# --- Tools ------------------------------------------------------------------

# The Python command used by ./bin/verify-model.sh. It uses only what Python
# ships with. Nothing is installed.
#PYTHON_BIN=python3

# 1 makes ./bin/setup.sh build an optional Python environment. Nothing in this
# repo needs it. It exists for people who want to inspect the weights directly.
#WITH_VENV=0
